  "global_analysis": "Saturday's global pulse: Wall Street ended a strong week on a split note — the S&P 500 fell 0.4% to 7,561.67 and the Nasdaq dropped 0.9% to 25,136.27 on Friday as Nvidia slid 2% and chip stocks tumbled, AMD down 8.8%, while the Dow edged up 0.1% to 52,347.55. For the week, all three indexes still gained roughly 2–2.5%, snapping four-week losing streaks. The 10-year Treasury yield climbed to 5.15%, gold ended the week lower at about $3,886 an ounce after starting it above $4,100, and Brent crude rose to $95.84 a barrel. Crypto sentiment stayed in extreme fear at 13. Fed officials struck a hawkish tone, with San Francisco's Daly saying rates may need to rise. Next week, all eyes turn to Nvidia's earnings on Tuesday — its first report since Jensen Huang resigned — plus US retail sales and Wednesday's FOMC minutes.",
